{
    "file": "Human_Behavior_Analysis_and_Micro_Expression.docx",
    "title": "Human Behavior Analysis & Micro-Expression",
    "duration": "5 Days",
    "overview": [
        "Understanding human behavior is an essential leadership capability. Leaders continuously interpret the attitudes, motivations, emotions, communication patterns, and reactions of employees, colleagues, stakeholders, and customers. Strong behavioral observation can improve communication, coaching, negotiation, conflict management, decision making, and relationship effectiveness when it is used carefully and without unsupported assumptions.",
        "This five-day course develops a structured approach to behavioral analysis using observable verbal, vocal, and non-verbal information. Participants examine behavioral baselines, context, body language, facial expressions, emotional signals, micro-expressions, interpersonal patterns, stress responses, rapport, and behavioral change. Particular emphasis is placed on avoiding simplistic interpretations: no single gesture, expression, or behavior is treated as proof of a person's thoughts, intentions, or truthfulness.",
        "Through observation exercises, video-based analysis, leadership scenarios, communication practice, and structured behavioral assessments, participants learn to combine multiple cues, test interpretations through questioning and context, and use behavioral insight to communicate and lead more effectively."
    ],
    "audience": [
        "Managers, senior managers, department heads, supervisors, team leaders, HR professionals, learning and development personnel, negotiators, interviewers, customer-facing leaders, project managers, and professionals whose roles require stronger observation, communication, interpersonal judgment, coaching, conflict management, or leadership effectiveness."
    ],
    "methodology": [
        "The course uses an experiential methodology combining short presentations, guided observation, video analysis, facial-expression exercises, behavioral-baseline activities, body-language interpretation exercises, leadership and workplace scenarios, role plays, questioning practice, case analysis, peer feedback, reflection, and action planning. Participants are repeatedly required to distinguish direct observation from interpretation and to validate conclusions through context and additional evidence."
    ],
    "competencies": [
        "Behavioral observation",
        "Contextual analysis",
        "Non-verbal communication awareness",
        "Facial-expression recognition",
        "Emotional cue recognition",
        "Active listening",
        "Behavioral questioning",
        "Interpersonal adaptability",
        "Leadership communication",
        "Conflict awareness",
        "Coaching and feedback",
        "Evidence-based behavioral judgment"
    ],
    "objectivesIntro": [
        "By the end of the course, participants will be able to:"
    ],
    "objectives": [
        "Explain the principles and limitations of behavioral analysis in professional settings.",
        "Distinguish observable behavior from assumptions, interpretations, and judgments.",
        "Establish behavioral baselines and recognize meaningful changes from normal patterns.",
        "Observe posture, gestures, orientation, movement, voice, and facial behavior systematically.",
        "Recognize common facial expressions associated with basic emotional states.",
        "Explain micro-expressions and apply appropriate caution when interpreting brief facial behavior.",
        "Combine verbal, vocal, facial, and body-language cues rather than relying on isolated signals.",
        "Use questioning and active listening to test behavioral interpretations.",
        "Adapt leadership and communication approaches according to observed interpersonal needs.",
        "Apply behavioral insight to coaching, conflict, negotiation, meetings, and difficult conversations.",
        "Avoid common biases and ethical errors in reading human behavior."
    ],
